\\begin{document}
\\maketitle

\\begin{abstract}
We investigate the hypothesis that observed logarithmic fermion mass ratios
are approximated by points on the regulator lattice of a totally real number
field. The anisotropy ratio $\\kappa=\\lambda_{\\max}/\\lambda_{\\min}$ of the
Gram matrix is constrained by results of Dobrowolski, Zimmert, and Friedman.
We conjecture that $\\kappa$ is uniformly bounded for totally real fields of
fixed degree (Conjecture~\\ref{conj:kappa}). Computational evidence from nine
totally real cubic fields gives $\\kappa\\in[1.4,12.8]$, supporting the
conjecture. Four fields exceed the spectrum obstruction threshold
$\\kappa\\geq 5.74$ and are not ruled out by the obstruction alone; generator
length matching provides the decisive further constraint.
\\end{abstract}

\\section{Introduction}
The nine charged Standard Model fermion masses span roughly thirteen orders
of magnitude. In logarithmic coordinates, approximate regularities suggest
an underlying arithmetic structure. A natural candidate is the regulator
lattice of a totally real number field: under the logarithmic embedding, the
unit group $\\OK^\\times$ becomes a lattice in a hyperplane of $\\R^r$~\\cite{neukirch}.
This paper studies geometric constraints that identifying fermion masses with
regulator lattice points would impose, using results of
Dobrowolski~\\cite{dobrowolski} and Zimmert--Friedman~\\cite{zimmert,friedman},
and connects to the spectrum obstruction of the companion paper~\\cite{paper7}.

\\section{Regulator Lattices}
Let $K$ be totally real of degree $r=[K:\\Q]$ with embeddings
$\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_r\\colon K\\hookrightarrow\\R$.
The logarithmic embedding is
\\begin{equation}
\\Lambda(\\epsilon)=\\bigl(\\log|\\sigma_1(\\epsilon)|,\\ldots,\\log|\\sigma_r(\\epsilon)|\\bigr).
\\end{equation}
The image lies in $H=\\{x\\in\\R^r:x_1+\\cdots+x_r=0\\}$.
Dirichlet's unit theorem gives a rank-$(r-1)$ lattice with covolume $R_K$.

\\begin{definition}[Anisotropy ratio]
With fundamental unit log vectors $v_i=\\Lambda(\\epsilon_i)$, Gram matrix
$G_{ij}=\\langle v_i,v_j\\rangle$, and eigenvalues
$0<\\lambda_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq\\lambda_{r-1}$, the \\emph{anisotropy ratio} is
$\\kappa(K)=\\lambda_{r-1}/\\lambda_1$.
\\end{definition}

\\section{Classical Bounds}

\\begin{theorem}[Dobrowolski~\\cite{dobrowolski}]
For any non-torsion unit $\\epsilon$,
$\\|\\Lambda(\\epsilon)\\|\\geq\\delta_r>0$ uniformly in the degree.
\\end{theorem}

\\begin{theorem}[Zimmert--Friedman~\\cite{zimmert,friedman}]
For totally real $K$ of degree $r$,
$a_r(\\log|D_K|)^{r-1}\\leq R_K\\leq b_r(\\log|D_K|)^{r-1}$.
\\end{theorem}

\\section{The Bounded Anisotropy Conjecture}

\\begin{conjecture}[Bounded anisotropy]\\label{conj:kappa}
For fixed $r$, there exists $C_r$ such that $\\kappa(K)\\leq C_r$
for every totally real field $K$ of degree $r$.
\\end{conjecture}

The heuristic motivation: large $\\kappa$ forces $\\lambda_1$ small,
but Dobrowolski forbids arbitrarily short non-zero lattice vectors.
Closing this argument rigorously is the main open problem.

\\section{Computational Evidence}
We surveyed nine totally real cubic fields by scanning $a+b\\alpha+c\\alpha^2$
with $|a|,|b|,|c|\\leq 6$ for units of norm $\\pm1$, then computed $\\kappa$
from Gram matrix eigenvalues. Code: \\texttt{code/kappa\\_cubic.py}.

\\begin{table}[h]
\\centering
\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\\toprule
Field & Units & $\\lambda_1$ & $\\lambda_2$ & $\\kappa$ & vs.\\ threshold \\\\
\\midrule
$x^3-3x+1$     & 17 & 2.548 &  7.644 &  3.000 & below \\\\
$x^3-x^2-2x+1$ & 28 & 6.513 & 10.302 &  1.582 & below \\\\
$x^3-4x+1$     & 13 & 2.279 & 29.211 & 12.817 & above \\\\
$x^3-x^2-3x+1$ & 19 & 7.257 & 10.281 &  1.417 & below \\\\
$x^3-5x+1$     & 12 & 3.581 & 27.089 &  7.564 & above \\\\
$x^3-2x^2-x+1$ & 32 & 5.880 & 14.088 &  2.396 & below \\\\
$x^3-6x+2$     &  6 & 4.636 & 20.965 &  4.523 & below \\\\
$x^3-x^2-4x+3$ & 14 & 2.172 & 21.544 &  9.921 & above \\\\
$x^3-3x^2-x+1$ & 19 & 1.685 & 19.675 & 11.673 & above \\\\
\\bottomrule
\\multicolumn{6}{l}{Threshold: $\\kappa\\geq 5.74$ for $N\\leq 6$.}
\\end{tabular}
\\caption{Anisotropy ratios for nine totally real cubic fields.
$\\kappa\\in[1.4,12.8]$; four exceed the obstruction threshold.}
\\label{tab:kappa}
\\end{table}

\\section{Connection to the Spectrum Obstruction}
The spectrum obstruction of~\\cite{paper7} requires $\\kappa\\geq 34.46/N$;
for $N\\leq 6$ this gives $\\kappa\\geq 5.74$. Four fields in
Table~\\ref{tab:kappa} exceed this threshold and are not ruled out by the
obstruction alone. The decisive further constraint is that unit generator
lengths must match observed fermion mass ratios. Numerical tests on
$x^4-x^3-4x^2+4x+1$ showed unit log norms do not match required generator
lengths (see \\texttt{code/verify\\_ckm\\_v2.py}).

\\section{Open Problems}
\\begin{enumerate}
\\item Prove or disprove Conjecture~\\ref{conj:kappa} for $r=3$.
\\item Extend the survey to $r=4$: is $\\kappa$ bounded for quartic fields?
\\item Find a totally real field whose unit generator lengths match fermion mass ratios.
\\item Determine whether any totally real field satisfies both constraints simultaneously.
\\end{enumerate}

\\section{Conclusion}
Computational evidence from nine cubic fields gives $\\kappa\\in[1.4,12.8]$,
supporting Conjecture~\\ref{conj:kappa}. Four fields exceed the spectrum
obstruction threshold; all are further constrained by the generator length
requirement. Finding a field satisfying both constraints simultaneously
is the central open problem.

